            of the concept of a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vector+space"} vector space
          %h3#what_is_a_vector_space What is a vector space?
          %p There are at least three distinct conceptual roles which vectors and vector spaces play in mathematics:
          %ol
            %li
              %p
                A vector is a
                = succeed "." do
                  %em column of numbers
            %li
              %p
                A vector is a
                = succeed "." do
                  %em direction in space
            %li
              %p
                A vector is an element of a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/module"} module
                over the base
                = succeed "/" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ring"} ring
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/field"} field
          %p The first of these may be thought of as motivating the notion of
          %ul
            %li
              %a{:href => "#KV2VectorSpace"}
                Kapranov-Voevodsky
                = succeed "-vector" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mn 2
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 2
          %p the second the notion of
          %ul
            %li
              %a{:href => "#BaezCrans2VectorSpaces"}
                Baez-Crans
                = succeed "-vector" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mn 2
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 2
          %p the third the notion of
          %ul
            %li
              %a{:href => "#AbstractApproach"}>
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mn 2
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 2
                \-Modules
              \.
          %h3#KV2VectorSpace
            Kapranov–Voevodsky
            = succeed "-vector" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 2
          %p
            These were introduced in
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "#KapranovVoevodsky91"} Kapranov &amp; Voevodsky 1991
          %p
            The idea here is that just as a vector space can be regarded as a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/module"} module
            over the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ground+field"} ground field
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi k
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k
            = succeed "-vector" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 2
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi W
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} W
            should be a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category"} category
            which is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monoidal+category+module"} monoidal category module
            with some nice properties (such as being an abelian category) over a suitable
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monoidal+category"} monoidal category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi V
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
            which plays the role of the categorified ground field. There is then an obvious
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bicategory"} bicategory
            of such module categories.
          %p
            In fact, Kapranov and Voevodsky defined a
            %strong
              Kapranov–Voevodsky
              = succeed "-vector" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mn 2
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 2
            as an abelian
            = succeed "-module" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:lspace => "0em", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} Vect
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Vect
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mo{:lspace => "0em", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} Vect
                    %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Vect^n
            for some
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
          %p
            While this definition makes a lot of sense it does not give an abstract characterization of 2-vector spaces. That is, it is hardly different to simply defining a 2-vector space as a category equivalent to
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi Vect
                      %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Vect^n
          %p
            Because Kapranov–Voevodsky
            = succeed "-vector" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 2
            = succeed "-vector" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 2
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cobordism"} cobordism
            categories to higher vector spaces).
          %p
            An example of a Kapranov–Voevodsky
            = succeed "-vector" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
