              %a{:href => "#BaezCrans04"} Baez &amp; Crans 2004
          %p
            A
            %strong
              Baez–Crans
              = succeed "-vector" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mn 2
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 2
            is defined as a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/internal+category"} category internal
            to
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Vect"} Vect
            %em infinitesimal directions
            of a structure, such as in higher
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+theory"} Lie theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Dold-Kan+correspondence"} Dold-Kan theorem
            by Brown and Higgins),
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/strict+omega-category"} strict omega-categories
            internal to
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:lspace => "0em", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} Vect
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Vect
            are equivalent to chain complexes in non-negative degree and can be regarded as strict
            = succeed "-" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Disc
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi k
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Disc(k)
            = succeed "-modules." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn ∞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \infty
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homological+algebra"} homological algebra
            and many of the structures appearing in higher
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+theory"} Lie theory
            – for instance the definition of
            = succeed "-" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi L
                      %mn ∞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L_\infty
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/L-infinity-algebra"} algebras
            = succeed "-vector" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn ∞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \infty
            = succeed "-vector" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn ∞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \infty
            = succeed "-vector" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn ∞
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \infty
          %p
            They were also independently introduced and studied
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "#Forrester-Barker"} Forrester-Barker (2004)
          %h2#AbstractApproach
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mn 2
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 2
            \-modules and
            = succeed "-linear" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 2
          %p
            It is possible to conceive of 2-vector spaces of the Kapranov–Voevodsky and Baez–Crans type from a single unified perspective. Namely, by regarding the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ground+field"} ground field
            itself as a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/discrete+category"} discrete category
            we can think of it as a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monoidal+category"} monoidal category
            = succeed "-module" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Disc
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi k
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Disc(k)
            = succeed "-modules" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi k
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k
            = succeed "-action" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi k
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mo{:lspace => "0em", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} Vect
                    %mi k
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Vect_k
            which are equivalent to chain complexes of vector spaces concentrated in degree 0 and 1.
          %p
            In other words, a Baez–Crans
            = succeed "-vector" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 2
            = succeed "-vector" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 2
          %p
            For
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi V
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
            a general symmetric
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/closed+monoidal+category"} closed monoidal category
            the full bicategory of all
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monoidal+category+modules"} monoidal category modules
            over
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi V
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
            is in general hard to get under control, but what is more tractable is the sub-bicategory which may be addressed as the bicategory of
            = succeed "-modules" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi V
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
            %em with basis
            namely the category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi V
                  %mo −
                  %mi Mod
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V-Mod
            in the sense of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/enriched+category+theory"} enriched category theory
            with
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                objects are categories
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi C
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/enriched+category"} enriched over
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi V
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/modules"} modules
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi Mod
                        %mi C
                      %mo :
                      %mo =
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                      %mi C
                      %mo ,
                      %mi V
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Mod_C := [C,V]
            %li
              %p
                morphisms
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi C
                      %mo →
                      %mi D
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C \to D
                are
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bimodules"} bimodules
                = succeed ";" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msup
                          %mi C
                          %mi op
                        %mo ⊗
                        %mi D
                        %mo →
                        %mi V
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C^{op}\otimes D \to V
            %li
              %p
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mn 2
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 2
                \-morphisms are natural transformations.
          %p
            Notice that all
            = succeed "-categories" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi V
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi Mod
                    %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Mod_C
            of modules over a
            = succeed "-category" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi V
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            are naturally
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/copower"} tensored
            over
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi V
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
            and hence are
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monoidal+category+modules"} monoidal category modules
            over
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi V
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi W
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} W
            (a
            = succeed "-module)" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi k
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi S
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
            such that
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi W
                    %mo ≃
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mi S
                    %mo ,
                    %mi k
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} W \simeq [S,k]
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monoidal+category+module"} monoidal category module
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi W
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} W
            over
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi V
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
            to be equipped with a basis by providing an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivalence"} equivalence
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi W
                    %mo ≃
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mi C
                    %mo ,
                    %mi V
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} W \simeq [C,V]
            = succeed "-category" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi V
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi V
                  %mo −
                  %mi Mod
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V-Mod
            is the category of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi V
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
            2-vector spaces with basis.
          %p All of the examples on this page are special cases of this one.
          %h3#enriched_categories
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:lspace => "0em", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} Vect
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Vect
            \-enriched categories
          %p
            According to the above a
            = succeed "-" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Vect
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Vect
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/enriched+category"} enriched category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            can be regarded as a basis for the
            = succeed "-module" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Vect
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Vect
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi Mod
                      %mi C
                    %mo =
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mi C
                    %mo ,
                    %mi Vect
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Mod_C = [C,Vect]
            = succeed "-enriched" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Vect
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Vect
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebroid"} algebroid
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebra"} algebra
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi Mod
                    %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Mod_C
            is the familiar category of modules over an algebra.
          %p
            Notice that, by the very definition of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Morita+equivalence"} Morita equivalence
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:lspace => "0em", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} Vect
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Vect
            = succeed "-vector" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 2
          %p
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Vect
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Vect
            \-enriched categories as models for 2-vector spaces appear in
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                Jacob Lurie,
                %em On the classification of topological field theories
                (
                %a{:href => "http://www.math.harvard.edu/~lurie/papers/cobordism.pdf"}> pdf
                ) (see example 1.2.4)
            %li
              %p
                B. Toën, G. Vezzosi,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em A note on Chern character, loop spaces and derived algebraic geometry
                = succeed "," do
                  %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/0804.1274"} arXiv
          %p
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mn 2
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 2
            \-vector spaces in the sub-bicategory of algebras (
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Vect
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Vect
            \-enriched categories with a single object), bimodules and intertwiners are discussed in
          %ul
            %li
              U. Schreiber,
              %em
                AQFT from
                = succeed "-functorial" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi n
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
              (
              %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/0806.1079"}> arXiv
              ) (appendix A)
          %p and
          %ul
            %li
              U. Schreiber and K. Waldorf,
              %em Connections on non-abelian gerbes and their holonomy
              (
              %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/0808.1923"}> arXiv
              )
          %p
            Some blog discussion of this point is at
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "http://golem.ph.utexas.edu/category/2007/11/2vectors_in_trondheim.html"} 2-Vectors in Trodheim
          %h3#enriched_categories_2
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Ch
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi Vect
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Ch(Vect)
            \-enriched categories
          %p
            More generally one can replace vector spaces by complexes of vector spaces and consider
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Ch
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi Vect
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:lspace => "0em", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} Mod
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Ch(Vect)\Mod
            as a model for the
            = succeed "-category" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 2
            = succeed "-vector" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 2
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dg-category"} dg-categories
          %p It is argued in
          %ul
            %li
              B. Toën, G. Vezzosi,
              = succeed "," do
                %em A note on Chern character, loop spaces and derived algebraic geometry
              = succeed "," do
                %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/0804.1274"} arXiv
          %p
            that the generalization from
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Vect
                  %mo{:lspace => "0em", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} Mod
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Vect\Mod
            to
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Ch
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi Vect
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:lspace => "0em", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} Mod
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Ch(Vect)\Mod
            is necessary to have a good notion of higher sheaves of sections of 2-vector bundles, i.e. of higher coherent sheaves.
          %h3#revisiting_kapranovvoevodsky_2vector_spaces Revisiting Kapranov–Voevodsky 2-vector spaces
          %p
            Upon further restriction of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:lspace => "0em", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} Vect
                  %mo{:lspace => "0em", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} Mod
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Vect\Mod
            to 2-vector spaces whose basis is a
            = succeed "," do
              %em discrete category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi S
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
            (or the
            = succeed "-enriched" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Vect
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Vect
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi S
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
            which has just the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ground+field"} ground field
            object sitting over each element of
            = succeed ")" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi S
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
            = succeed "-modules" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Vect
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Vect
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi S
                  %mo ,
                  %mi Vect
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mo =
                  %msub
                    %mi Mod
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi k
                        %mi n
                  %mo ≃
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi Vect
                  %msup
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  [S, Vect] = Mod_{k^n} \simeq (Vect)^n
          %p
            (where
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi k
                    %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k^n
            denotes the algebra of diagonal
            = succeed "-matrices)." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                    %mo ×
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n\times n
            = succeed "-vector" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 2
          %h3#elgueta_vector_spaces
            Elgueta
            = succeed "-vector" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 2
          %p Another notion of 2-vector space which also includes Kaparanov–Voevodsky as particular instances is given in
          %ul
            %li
              Josep Elgueta,
              %em Generalized 2-vector spaces and general linear 2-groups
              (
              %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/math/0606472"}> arXiv
              )
          %p
            The idea is to categorify the construction of a vector space as the space of finite linear combinations of elements in any set
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi S
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi S
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            = succeed "-linear" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi k
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            = succeed "-vector" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 2
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            is discrete. In some cases this gives nonabelian and even non-
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Karoubian+category"} Karoubian
            (i.e., nonidempotent complete) categories. This is the case, for instance, when we take as
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            the one-object category defined by the additive monoid of natural numbers. The 2-vector space this category generates can be identified with the category of free
            = succeed "-modules," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi k
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mi T
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k[T]
          %h3#infinitedimensional_kv_2vector_spaces Infinite-dimensional K-V 2-vector spaces
          %p
            We can regard the objects of the
            = succeed "-dimensional" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            = succeed "-vector" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 2
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi Vect
                    %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Vect^n
            – which are
            = succeed "-tuples" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            elements. This has an obvious generalization to vector bundles over any topological space – in terms of modules these are the finitely generated projective modules of the algebra of continuous functions on this space. So categories of vector bundles can be regarded as infinite-dimensional 2-vector spaces. For the case that the underlying topological space is a
            %em measure space
            such infinite dimensional K-V 2-vector spaces have been studied in
          %ul
            %li
              John C. Baez, Aristide Baratin,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Laurent+Freidel"} Laurent Freidel
              %em Infinite-dimensional representations of 2-groups
              (
              %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/0812.4969"}> arXiv
              )
          %h3#using_a_modular_tensor_category Using a modular tensor category
          %p
            The relevance of module categories as models for 2-vector spaces was apparently first realized in the context of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/conformal+field+theory"} conformal field theory
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi V
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
            in question is a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/modular+tensor+category"} modular tensor category
            %em all
            = succeed "-module" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi V
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi Mod
                    %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Mod_A
            for
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            some one-object
            = succeed "-enriched" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi V
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
            = succeed ")" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi V
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
          %ul
            %li
              V. Ostrik,
              %em Module Categories, weak Hopf Algebras and Modular Invariants
              (
              %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/math.QA/0111139"}> arXiv
              ,
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "http://golem.ph.utexas.edu/string/archives/000717.html"} blog
          %h2#2modules_as_modules_over_a_2ring 2-Modules as modules over a 2-ring
          %p
            One can go further and derive the identification of 2-modules and 2-linear maps with algebras and bimodules from a more fundamental notion of modules over
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2-rings"} 2-rings
            %em
              %a{:href => "2-rig#CompatiblyMonoidalPresentableCategories"} 2-ring – Compatibly monoidal presentable categories
            for more details.
          %h2#remark_on_the_different_notions_of_vector_spaces
            Remark on the different notions of
            = succeed "-vector" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 2
          %p
            As the above list shows, there are 2-vector spaces of very different kind. There is not
            %em the
            notion of 2-vector space which is the universal right answer. Different notions of vector spaces are applicable and useful in different situations. This can be regarded as nothing but a more pronounced incarnation of the fact that already ordinary vector space appear in different flavors which are useful in different situations (real vector spaces, complex vector spaces, vector spaces over a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/finite+field"} finite field
          %p
            For instance
            = succeed "-module" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Disc
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi k
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Disc(k)
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+theory"} Lie theory
            but 2-bundles with fibers
            = succeed "-module" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Disc
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi k
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Disc(k)
          %ul
            %li
              %span.newWikiWord>
                Nils. A. Baas
                %a{:href => "/nlab/new/Nils.+A.+Baas"} ?
              , Marcel Bökstedt, Tore August Kro,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Two-Categorical Bundles and Their Classifying Spaces
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/math/0612549"} arXiv
          %h2#hilbert_spaces
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mn 2
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 2
            \-Hilbert spaces
          %p
            2-vector spaces have to a large extent been motivated by and applied in (2-dimensional)
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+field+theory"} quantum field theory
          %p
            2-Hilbert spaces as a
            = succeed "-" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:lspace => "0em", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} Hilb
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Hilb
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/enriched+category"} enriched categories
            with some extra properties were discribed in
          %ul
            %li
              John Baez,
              %em Higher-Dimensional Algebra II: 2-Hilbert Spaces
              (
              %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/q-alg/9609018"}> arXiv
              ) .
          %p
            In applications one often assumes these 2-Hilbert spaces to be
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/semisimple+algebra"} semisimple
            in which case such a 2-Hilbert space is a Kapranov–Voevodsky
            = succeed "-vector" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 2
          %p A review of these ideas of 2-Hilbert spaces as well as applications of 2-Hilbert spaces to finite group representation theory are in
          %ul
            %li
              Bruce Bartlett,
              %em On unitary 2-representations of finite groups and topological quantum field theory
              (
              %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/0901.3975"}> arXiv
              )
          %h2#properties Properties
          %h3#tannaka_duality Tannaka duality
          %div
            %p
